A||||9||fierocd||popping noise in radio||||1||
Z||000000||fierocd||06-03-2001||07:50 PM||||ever since I put my head unit, amp., and new speakers in my 86 GT, every time I hit the brakes I can hear a popping noise comming from the speakers.  I have a Clarion head unit, Kicker impulse amp. and front speakers, and MB Quart rear speakers.  does anyone know what may cause this.  I just ran new ground wires for both the radio and amp. thinking that was the problem, but that didn't make a difference.  Also, my Pontiac lights on the back don't light up anymore, I don't know if this has anything to do with it.  Any help is appreciated.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||06-03-2001||08:22 PM||||First, Where are the RCA lines that go from the radio to the Amp? If they run down the sides by the doors you could be picking up the noise from the wires. The RCA lines could also be of poor quality. The engine ground may have come loose. This is the braided wire/cable that goes from the engine block to the right hinge bracket on the decklid. Is the power for the amp coming straight from the battery? ARe all the connections tight on the back of the alternator? These are some things I have seen in the past cause noise and "pop" problems. I think your unlit PONTIAC light is a seperate problem. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierocd||06-03-2001||08:30 PM||||The RCA's are running alongside the drivers seat on the right side, I don't have them hidden anywhere so I dont think that is the problem.  The RCA's are Rockford Fosgate's, not my favorite but they are supposed to be good.  The power for the amp. is running off the power for the radio, running a seperate power wire was my next solution.  I'll check the alternator and ground strap next.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierocd||06-03-2001||10:14 PM||||I checked the ground strap on the engine, it was loose so I tightened it, still pops.  All the connections on the alternator seem tight.  Anything else that could cause this?||||reg||1
Z||000004||DecadenceR||06-03-2001||10:30 PM||||Pops in a radio can be almsot anything. Loose connections are a biggie. Also, any wires, power or ground, can make you pick up noise. Once I ran a power wire in a 92 Celica near the horn. Every time the horn was used, we could hear it in the speakers. We moved the wire to the other side and it was fine.<BR>But I'd definitely run your power directly to the battery before anything else. And make sure any grounds you hooked up are to bare metal - seatbelt anchors usually work very wekk for this.<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||06-03-2001||11:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierocd:<BR><B>The power for the amp. is running off the power for the radio, running a seperate power wire was my next solution.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's the problem, The amp needs to be hooked straight to the battery. (With a fuse of course). You can get an "Amp wiring kit" and most Pepboys and Trak Auto stores. Most Amps require this so the drivers don't get "current starved". The amp wanting the current to drive the speakers will pick up any other changes in the harness. I bet it pops on turn signals, brake lights, hazard lights, and probably makes a whirring noise thru the speakers when your headlights are turned off and on.<BR>Once hooked to the battery, your popping will stop. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierocd||06-04-2001||04:05 PM||||It only makes noise when I hit the brakes, the only other noise it makes is an alternator whine.  I ran a new power wire directly from the battery to the amp. and that did nothing, it still pops.  I'm going to try to run new power wires for the head unit now and see if that works.  Any other ideas would be helpfull.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||06-04-2001||05:06 PM||||my radio 'pops' just like yours with sigs, brakes. I dont have any amp. just an aftermarket tape player/aftermarket replacement speakers. all my connections are good too ?????||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||06-04-2001||05:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierocd:<BR><B>I ran a new power wire directly from the battery to the amp. and that did nothing, Any other ideas would be helpfull.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What guage wire did you use for your amp lead? I recommend 10 guage minimum with the same size wire for the ground. Make sure it is very well grounded, bolt it to stripped metal and solder that sucker if you gotta.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierocd||06-04-2001||05:30 PM||||Actually, I switched amps.  I put in a cheap Pyramid 600watt and it worked fine.  I guess it was my amp. making the problems and not any of the wiring.  I'm going to try my Kicker amp. in my friends car and see if I get any similar problems.  Thanks for all the help, I was going to try a different amp. first but I figured that couldn't be the problem.||||reg||1
